1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Its home is the most beautiful building in Washington.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:13,000
A treasure chest of 144 million items stored on 650 miles of shelves.

3
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
From handwritten manuscripts of American presidents...

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Here we have the diaries of George Washington.

5
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
To cat scans of Stradivarius violins...

6
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:29,000
We can study the inside of it, the air on the inside, the density of the wood.

7
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:33,000
A staff of 4,000 works to reveal secrets that have been lost

8
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:37,000
for centuries and preserve them in the digital age.

9
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:41,140
And it's all there for you.

10
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:44,940
Now, the Library of Congress on Modern Marvels.

11
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:03,540
This library is perhaps the most misunderstood in the world.

12
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:05,740
Each year, hundreds of thousands of tourists

13
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:08,740
enter the Library of Congress Jefferson Building

14
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:11,140
and stand in awe.

15
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:15,380
But after they snap a few photos and get back on the bus,

16
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:17,080
they may leave with the misconception

17
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:19,520
that this is simply where members of Congress

18
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:22,260
get to borrow their books.

19
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:25,800
In reality, it is so much more.

20
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:28,500
It's the biggest collection of knowledge

21
00:01:28,501 --> 00:01:30,640
in all languages and formats.

22
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:33,140
That is to say, it's the biggest collection of maps, movies,

23
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:36,340
music, as well as books in English.

24
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:38,640
And most of the books are in other languages.

25
00:01:38,740 --> 00:01:41,840
So we have books in 470 languages.

26
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:44,740
We serve the Congress and by extension, the government.

27
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:47,160
But at the same time, the same information

28
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:51,360
is available to the general public.

29
00:01:51,460 --> 00:01:53,360
Nowhere else in the world can a researcher

30
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:57,560
view the earliest works of Benjamin Franklin.

31
00:01:57,561 --> 00:02:01,500
inspect a first draft musical composition of Mozart,

32
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:05,700
or screen the original negatives of classic American films,

33
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:08,300
like Frankenstein.

34
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:10,000
If you hold the film up to the light

35
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:11,500
and look through the film, you could

36
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:14,620
see these little white spots where the splices could

37
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:15,620
actually be seen.

38
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:17,120
And only the original camera negative

39
00:02:17,220 --> 00:02:19,620
would have so many splices.

40
00:02:19,720 --> 00:02:21,760
For historians, pop culture fanatics,

41
00:02:21,860 --> 00:02:26,560
and the just plain curious, this library has no peer,

42
00:02:26,561 --> 00:02:31,360
mission is critical. We are rescuing the United States

43
00:02:31,361 --> 00:02:34,100
culture. When you think about it, if this

44
00:02:34,101 --> 00:02:37,600
disappears, our future generations will really know more

45
00:02:37,601 --> 00:02:42,400
about the Greeks because of stone tablet technology than they will

46
00:02:42,401 --> 00:02:45,520
know about us. And that's what we're trying to

47
00:02:45,521 --> 00:02:48,520
change. To accomplish this daunting task,

48
00:02:48,521 --> 00:02:53,960
4,000 library staffers work to sort, categorize, preserve, and

49
00:02:53,961 --> 00:02:59,200
and protect 144 million items stored in a complex of five

50
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:01,600
main buildings.

51
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:04,740
On Capitol Hill, the Jefferson, Madison, and Adams

52
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:07,680
buildings house many collections,

53
00:03:07,780 --> 00:03:12,140
and most of the staff who perform tasks from barcoding,

54
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:15,140
to copyright registration, to preservation.

55
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:20,720
If we understand how things become damaged,

56
00:03:20,820 --> 00:03:23,020
then that helps us to understand how to prevent

57
00:03:23,021 --> 00:03:25,420
damage or repair it later.

58
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:28,600
30 miles northeast at Fort Meade, Maryland,

59
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:31,360
massive new storage modules house

60
00:03:31,460 --> 00:03:35,240
several more million items in climate control stacks 30 feet

61
00:03:35,340 --> 00:03:39,140
high, longer than football fields.

62
00:03:39,240 --> 00:03:42,840
We have not lost an item yet.

63
00:03:42,940 --> 00:03:45,080
75 miles southwest of the capital

64
00:03:45,180 --> 00:03:48,620
in Culpeper, Virginia, the Packard campus for video

65
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:52,780
and audio preservation is now the home of America's motion

66
00:03:52,781 --> 00:03:56,380
pictures treasures. These are state of the art vaults.

67
00:03:56,480 --> 00:03:59,620
We store original nitrate negatives of some of the greatest

68
00:03:59,720 --> 00:04:01,820
classics of Hollywood history here.

69
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:06,220
The heart of the Library of Congress

70
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:09,940
is the Jefferson Building's Great Hall, named after the

71
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:12,740
president who believed the expansion of knowledge was

72
00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:15,500
critical to the work of congressmen and the growth

73
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:20,040
of American ideals. Two centuries ago,

74
00:04:20,041 --> 00:04:23,140
This building didn't exist.

75
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:25,640
The first library collections were stored in the Capitol

76
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:26,740
itself.

77
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:29,080
During the War of 1812, British troops

78
00:04:29,180 --> 00:04:32,580
set fire to the structure, destroying thousands of books.

79
00:04:32,680 --> 00:04:34,520
Congress replenished its collection

80
00:04:34,620 --> 00:04:37,220
by purchasing Thomas Jefferson's personal library,

81
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:43,300
6,487 volumes for $23,950.

82
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:44,960
There was a narrow vote in the Congress,

83
00:04:45,060 --> 00:04:49,340
whether to buy it or not, because much of the New

84
00:04:49,341 --> 00:04:55,000
New England delegation said, why buy this farmer from Virginia's library

85
00:04:55,001 --> 00:04:57,660
which has books on every conceivable subject?

86
00:04:57,820 --> 00:05:02,340
And Jefferson said that there's no subject on which a legislator

87
00:05:02,341 --> 00:05:07,560
in our type of republic may not need to have information on.

88
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:10,740
And his own library contained books in 16 languages.

89
00:05:11,280 --> 00:05:14,560
He was studying Arabic in his advancing years.

90
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:18,440
So we had the first English language translation of the Koran,

91
00:05:18,441 --> 00:05:23,540
all kinds of things. During the next century, 800

92
00:05:23,541 --> 00:05:28,480
tons of books, maps, and manuscripts accumulated and needed a home of their own.

93
00:05:28,481 --> 00:05:34,620
Congress built them, a palace. This building is generally considered

94
00:05:34,621 --> 00:05:38,668
the most elaborately embellished public building in America.

95
00:05:38,669 --> 00:05:40,760
And all you have to do to prove

96
00:05:40,761 --> 00:05:43,345
that point is look around a little bit.

97
00:05:43,346 --> 00:05:46,460
An extraordinary collection of panoramic photos

98
00:05:46,461 --> 00:05:49,400
documents the construction of what is now known as the

99
00:05:49,401 --> 00:05:54,100
Library's Jefferson Building. The $6 million structure was

100
00:05:54,101 --> 00:05:58,800
completed in 1897 and hailed as a masterpiece.

101
00:05:58,801 --> 00:06:00,900
It was one of the first buildings in Washington to

102
00:06:00,901 --> 00:06:05,820
make use of electric light. Every artistic detail has

103
00:06:05,821 --> 00:06:11,620
significance. Many of the sculpted figures

104
00:06:11,621 --> 00:06:15,860
hold torches of knowledge. Now, that torch, that fire,

105
00:06:15,861 --> 00:06:17,860
represents the active mind.

106
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:20,260
It also represents the human imagination.

107
00:06:26,440 --> 00:06:29,140
Nearly a century later, the Jefferson Building

108
00:06:29,240 --> 00:06:31,440
underwent extensive renovation.

109
00:06:31,540 --> 00:06:35,520
Historic paintings damaged by soot were given new life.

110
00:06:35,620 --> 00:06:38,380
Technical upgrades were made virtually invisible to visitors.

111
00:06:41,220 --> 00:06:45,120
If you look above, you can see the air conditioning vents.

112
00:06:45,121 --> 00:06:48,860
we cut out the center of one of the rosettes there,

113
00:06:48,960 --> 00:06:52,020
and we installed an air conditioning vent.

114
00:06:52,120 --> 00:06:54,760
And down lower, you can see where we cut out the center

115
00:06:54,860 --> 00:06:59,360
of a rosette, and we installed a fire sprinkler

116
00:06:59,460 --> 00:07:01,440
in that location.

117
00:07:01,540 --> 00:07:03,680
The Jefferson Building today remains the center

118
00:07:03,780 --> 00:07:06,980
point of the library complex, housing book stacks,

119
00:07:07,080 --> 00:07:10,320
collections, and meeting rooms.

120
00:07:10,420 --> 00:07:13,020
Adjacent to the Great Hall is the library's core research

121
00:07:13,120 --> 00:07:14,320
area.

122
00:07:14,321 --> 00:07:16,220
The main reading room.

123
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:19,660
At the Library of Congress, you don't go to the books.

124
00:07:19,760 --> 00:07:21,160
They come to you.

125
00:07:21,260 --> 00:07:22,260
Why?

126
00:07:22,360 --> 00:07:25,760
Because items in the collection are truly treasures.

127
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:29,160
And they're treated as such.

128
00:07:29,260 --> 00:07:31,800
Take, for example, the original copy of Thomas Jefferson's

129
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:34,140
Koran.

130
00:07:34,240 --> 00:07:38,080
Reaching its home and that of most library collections

131
00:07:38,180 --> 00:07:42,880
requires climbing century-old staircases,

132
00:07:42,881 --> 00:07:45,920
passing through camera-monitored hallways,

133
00:07:46,020 --> 00:07:48,920
and unlocking steel-doored vaults designed to protect

134
00:07:49,020 --> 00:07:51,120
their contents with state-of-the-art technology.

135
00:07:54,020 --> 00:07:56,020
My vault is temperature controlled.

136
00:07:56,120 --> 00:07:57,420
The light is controlled.

137
00:07:57,520 --> 00:07:59,120
The air is filtered.

138
00:07:59,220 --> 00:08:00,940
We have alarms for humidity.

139
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:02,140
We have alarms for water.

140
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:03,640
We have alarms for intrusion.

141
00:08:03,740 --> 00:08:06,540
We have alarms for things I'm not allowed to talk about.

142
00:08:06,640 --> 00:08:10,440
Books like to live in a constant state.

143
00:08:10,540 --> 00:08:12,280
They don't like fluctuation.

144
00:08:12,281 --> 00:08:14,280
They like it a little colder than humans,

145
00:08:14,281 --> 00:08:16,520
and that's just fine.

146
00:08:16,521 --> 00:08:20,080
Thomas Jefferson never wrote his name in his books.

147
00:08:20,081 --> 00:08:25,020
So how do we know this copy of the Koran actually belonged to him?

148
00:08:25,021 --> 00:08:29,200
What he would do is go and look for a large capital letter

149
00:08:29,201 --> 00:08:31,540
that's often printed at the bottom of a page

150
00:08:31,541 --> 00:08:33,440
in 18th century books, so that a binder

151
00:08:33,441 --> 00:08:34,940
can put the book together.

152
00:08:34,941 --> 00:08:37,740
Jefferson would go to the T signature

153
00:08:37,741 --> 00:08:40,840
and put his letter J next to it for Thomas Jefferson.

154
00:08:40,841 --> 00:08:44,140
So the only way that Jefferson would ever mark his books

155
00:08:44,240 --> 00:08:47,180
is this very subtle, very sophisticated,

156
00:08:47,280 --> 00:08:52,080
very kind of endearing initial of Thomas Jefferson.

157
00:08:52,180 --> 00:08:54,120
The rare book vaults of the Jefferson building

158
00:08:54,220 --> 00:08:57,860
may be considered the library's holiest of holies.

159
00:08:57,960 --> 00:09:01,460
Benjamin Franklin's Poor Richard's Almanacs are here,

160
00:09:01,560 --> 00:09:04,360
showing his entrepreneurial genius.

161
00:09:04,460 --> 00:09:07,100
Franklin needed to outsell an already popular almanac

162
00:09:07,200 --> 00:09:10,000
by Titan Leeds.

163
00:09:10,001 --> 00:09:13,340
So Franklin predicted the date of Leeds' death.

164
00:09:13,440 --> 00:09:16,600
When the date passed, Leeds cried foul,

165
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:20,640
and Franklin laughed all the way to the bank.

166
00:09:20,740 --> 00:09:23,420
Franklin countered by saying that he thought it was horrible

167
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:26,480
that this poor Leeds family was suffering the death of Titan

168
00:09:26,580 --> 00:09:28,160
Leeds, and here are these people writing in,

169
00:09:28,260 --> 00:09:30,720
pretending to be this poor, dead publisher.

170
00:09:30,820 --> 00:09:33,920
This goes back and forth, whipping up just enough

171
00:09:34,020 --> 00:09:36,820
enthusiasm that poor Richard's almanac takes hold.

172
00:09:36,821 --> 00:09:40,520
And by the time Leeds actually does die,

173
00:09:40,620 --> 00:09:44,120
it allows poor Richard to publish a note saying,

174
00:09:44,220 --> 00:09:46,160
thank goodness the publisher of that other almanac

175
00:09:46,260 --> 00:09:49,560
have finally admitted to the death of Mr. Leeds.

176
00:09:49,660 --> 00:09:51,800
The rare book vaults of the Library of Congress

177
00:09:51,900 --> 00:09:55,480
contain more than 700,000 volumes.

178
00:09:55,580 --> 00:09:59,840
One of the most valued was published in 1610,

179
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:05,020
Galileo's Sidereus Nuncius, or Starry Messenger,

180
00:10:05,021 --> 00:10:09,800
containing sketches of the moon as seen through a telescope.

181
00:10:09,860 --> 00:10:13,100
We imagine that he likely drew this while observing,

182
00:10:13,160 --> 00:10:16,000
and then went and had this image cut so that it could be printed

183
00:10:16,060 --> 00:10:17,760
and included in his publication.

184
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:21,140
And in fact, what really makes The Starry Messenger such an

185
00:10:21,200 --> 00:10:23,780
important publication is that there are multiple images

186
00:10:23,840 --> 00:10:26,200
of the moon.

187
00:10:26,280 --> 00:10:29,320
In another vault, a scrapbook,

188
00:10:29,380 --> 00:10:31,580
famed for its extraordinary collection of personal

189
00:10:31,581 --> 00:10:36,320
memorabilia from legendary magician Eric Weiss, better

190
00:10:36,420 --> 00:10:41,560
known by his stage name, Harry Houdini.

191
00:10:41,660 --> 00:10:46,800
What we're seeing is a note from a 14-year-old Houdini who

192
00:10:46,900 --> 00:10:50,360
has left home to look for work and, as an afterthought,

193
00:10:50,460 --> 00:10:54,640
is writing his mother to say, oh, by the way,

194
00:10:54,740 --> 00:10:57,380
I'm on my way to a job.

195
00:10:57,480 --> 00:10:59,800
You probably won't see me for about a year.

196
00:10:59,801 --> 00:11:04,040
your truant son, and then I think one of only two known

197
00:11:04,140 --> 00:11:07,580
signatures in his real name, Eric Weiss.

198
00:11:07,680 --> 00:11:10,480
Researchers view volumes from the rare book collection

199
00:11:10,580 --> 00:11:13,480
in a room adjacent to the vaults.

200
00:11:13,580 --> 00:11:15,880
But those who read books from the general collection

201
00:11:15,980 --> 00:11:19,460
do so under the watchful eye of a young, clean-shaven,

202
00:11:19,560 --> 00:11:23,260
Abraham Lincoln, in the main reading room.

203
00:11:23,360 --> 00:11:25,920
Card catalogs that once occupied the space

204
00:11:25,921 --> 00:11:30,860
space have been replaced by computerized search systems.

205
00:11:30,960 --> 00:11:33,420
Eventually, when we started digitizing,

206
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:37,360
and it took years for people because you had to key it in.

207
00:11:37,460 --> 00:11:39,660
We're talking 1960s.

208
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:42,340
And we still have our card catalog.

209
00:11:42,440 --> 00:11:44,340
It's just off the reading room, and we still

210
00:11:44,440 --> 00:11:47,080
use it all the time because there are things in it that

211
00:11:47,180 --> 00:11:50,480
are not in the online catalog.

212
00:11:50,580 --> 00:11:52,180
Books requested by researchers

213
00:11:52,280 --> 00:11:55,520
may be retrieved within minutes if housed in century-old stacks

214
00:11:55,521 --> 00:11:59,060
of the Jefferson Building.

215
00:11:59,160 --> 00:12:00,920
There are nine levels.

216
00:12:01,020 --> 00:12:02,220
We're on the lowest level.

217
00:12:02,320 --> 00:12:04,120
There are eight levels above us.

218
00:12:04,220 --> 00:12:08,860
In the original stacks, roughly 90,000 volumes per level.

219
00:12:08,960 --> 00:12:13,580
So about 810,000 volumes on the nine levels,

220
00:12:13,680 --> 00:12:16,380
and a mirror image, the same thing on the north side

221
00:12:16,480 --> 00:12:18,780
of the building.

222
00:12:18,880 --> 00:12:21,720
But other books may travel 30 miles to Capitol Hill

223
00:12:21,820 --> 00:12:25,520
from the Fort Meade storage facilities.

224
00:12:25,521 --> 00:12:29,520
where forklift operators know exactly where to find

225
00:12:29,521 --> 00:12:34,520
or return a single book among millions.

226
00:12:34,521 --> 00:12:36,520
It's 90 degrees outside today,

227
00:12:36,521 --> 00:12:38,520
but in here it's 50 degrees Fahrenheit,

228
00:12:38,521 --> 00:12:40,520
30% relative humidity,

229
00:12:40,521 --> 00:12:43,520
which is really good for paper-based products.

230
00:12:43,521 --> 00:12:45,520
We have paper-based products that,

231
00:12:45,521 --> 00:12:49,520
in a normal environment, would probably last about 40 years,

232
00:12:49,521 --> 00:12:51,520
and when you put them into this 50-degree,

233
00:12:51,521 --> 00:12:53,520
30% relative humidity environment,

234
00:12:53,521 --> 00:12:57,220
the life can be increased about sixfold.

235
00:12:57,320 --> 00:12:59,920
Books requested from Fort Meade are delivered twice daily

236
00:13:00,020 --> 00:13:03,020
to the Jefferson Building.

237
00:13:03,120 --> 00:13:05,860
Once the books are found and retrieved downstairs,

238
00:13:05,960 --> 00:13:08,860
they're put on this conveyor system, and they come up here.

239
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:12,180
These are actually books that will be taken off,

240
00:13:12,280 --> 00:13:14,640
and they will be delivered to the person at their seat

241
00:13:14,740 --> 00:13:16,180
in the reading room.

242
00:13:18,280 --> 00:13:20,580
It's doubtful that there's a single more beautiful room

243
00:13:20,581 --> 00:13:24,280
in the world in which to simply read a book.

244
00:13:24,380 --> 00:13:26,420
Thomas Jefferson likely never imagined

245
00:13:26,520 --> 00:13:30,080
such a luxurious home for his nearly 6,500 books

246
00:13:30,180 --> 00:13:31,520
and the millions more to come.

247
00:13:35,320 --> 00:13:37,340
What do I think Thomas Jefferson would think

248
00:13:37,440 --> 00:13:38,540
of this building?

249
00:13:38,640 --> 00:13:42,240
I think he'd, first of all, think it was a bit much.

250
00:13:42,340 --> 00:13:44,640
But I think he would grow into it.

251
00:13:44,740 --> 00:13:48,740
I think he would think that he'd started a good thing

252
00:13:48,741 --> 00:13:53,740
and how nicely for it all to end up.

253
00:13:53,840 --> 00:13:57,380
Actual books make up only 21% of the Library of Congress'

254
00:13:57,480 --> 00:13:58,380
collection.

255
00:13:58,480 --> 00:14:01,280
Other treasures, from manuscripts, to maps, to

256
00:14:01,380 --> 00:14:05,080
photographs, to musical instruments, have a secure home

257
00:14:05,180 --> 00:14:08,100
in the library's Madison Building, where scientists

258
00:14:08,200 --> 00:14:11,500
race the clock to develop new methods of preserving priceless

259
00:14:11,600 --> 00:14:14,200
treasures before they literally fall apart.

260
00:14:18,740 --> 00:14:24,740
The Library of Congress is also known as America's Library.

261
00:14:24,741 --> 00:14:28,740
Its nearly 144 million items are available for viewing in the

262
00:14:28,741 --> 00:14:32,740
luxurious reading rooms in Washington, D.C., and

263
00:14:32,741 --> 00:14:34,740
increasingly online.

264
00:14:34,741 --> 00:14:37,740
Each day, thousands of pages and images are scanned and

265
00:14:37,741 --> 00:14:43,740
digitized for viewing on loc.gov, the library's website,

266
00:14:43,741 --> 00:14:46,740
which now makes available more than 15 million items of the

267
00:14:46,741 --> 00:14:52,380
of the library collection. Since his appointment as Librarian

268
00:14:52,480 --> 00:14:56,880
of Congress in 1987, Dr. James Billington has been preparing

269
00:14:56,980 --> 00:15:01,580
the 200-year-old institution for the 21st century.

270
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:07,300
Our 15,300,000 items of American history and culture

271
00:15:07,400 --> 00:15:10,100
are not just books. They're not even primarily books.

272
00:15:10,200 --> 00:15:13,800
They're one-of-a-kind things. American memory, we call it

273
00:15:13,801 --> 00:15:16,700
we call it sometimes, as well as the National Digital Library.

274
00:15:16,880 --> 00:15:21,160
You can get that out to people where they live in the format

275
00:15:21,161 --> 00:15:25,680
in which our culture is increasingly digesting

276
00:15:25,681 --> 00:15:27,420
and expressing knowledge.

277
00:15:27,640 --> 00:15:29,680
That is to say, in the digital format.

278
00:15:30,660 --> 00:15:32,620
Across the street from the Jefferson Building

279
00:15:32,621 --> 00:15:35,640
of the Library of Congress is the structure named after

280
00:15:35,641 --> 00:15:39,520
James Madison, home to many library collections

281
00:15:39,521 --> 00:15:43,520
and research scientists working to use digital tools

282
00:15:43,521 --> 00:15:45,220
to solve preservation dilemmas.

283
00:15:48,220 --> 00:15:51,920
Every day, 14,000 new items arrive by mail

284
00:15:52,020 --> 00:15:54,000
to be sorted for the collection.

285
00:15:54,100 --> 00:15:55,500
Some are donated.

286
00:15:55,600 --> 00:15:57,600
Others are purchased.

287
00:15:57,700 --> 00:16:02,700
In the last fiscal year, 2008, we spent about $18 million

288
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:06,540
and purchased over a million separate items.

289
00:16:06,640 --> 00:16:08,740
As home of the US Copyright Office,

290
00:16:08,840 --> 00:16:11,340
the library also receives cartloads of material

291
00:16:11,341 --> 00:16:14,680
copyright registration.

292
00:16:14,780 --> 00:16:18,340
We get 8,000 to 12,000 pieces of mail every day.

293
00:16:18,440 --> 00:16:20,980
This is probably from somebody's garage band.

294
00:16:21,080 --> 00:16:23,980
They're going ahead and they're producing a CD in their basement.

295
00:16:24,080 --> 00:16:25,880
They want to copyright their songs.

296
00:16:25,980 --> 00:16:27,880
Who knows what the future will be for them.

297
00:16:31,400 --> 00:16:34,300
Copyright specialists make sure the names of applicants

298
00:16:34,400 --> 00:16:37,900
match those of artists actually listed on the work.

299
00:16:37,901 --> 00:16:41,940
For motion picture specialists, that means sneak previews only

300
00:16:42,040 --> 00:16:42,940
amount to credit verification.

301
00:16:46,840 --> 00:16:48,740
Those who review musical compositions

302
00:16:48,840 --> 00:16:51,040
need to know if a string of notes qualifies

303
00:16:51,140 --> 00:16:54,860
as a copyrightable tune.

304
00:16:54,960 --> 00:16:57,760
Everyone in this division has a music background.

305
00:16:57,860 --> 00:16:59,260
I was a music major in college.

306
00:16:59,360 --> 00:17:01,460
I was a harpist.

307
00:17:01,560 --> 00:17:04,200
The Copyright Office processes more than a half million

308
00:17:04,201 --> 00:17:08,200
registrations each year, resulting in a catalog, soon

309
00:17:08,300 --> 00:17:12,500
to be digitized, of nearly 50 million cards.

310
00:17:12,600 --> 00:17:15,140
The library doesn't have a copy of every book published

311
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:19,140
in the United States. The Copyright Office keeps records

312
00:17:19,240 --> 00:17:22,060
of registration, but not every item.

313
00:17:24,960 --> 00:17:26,760
As the heart of the library's collection

314
00:17:26,860 --> 00:17:31,560
is material printed or written on paper, from books to manuscripts

315
00:17:31,561 --> 00:17:33,260
to maps and photographs.

316
00:17:33,860 --> 00:17:37,020
Its biggest challenge is to keep the items in pristine condition

317
00:17:37,021 --> 00:17:40,640
and avoid something like this.

318
00:17:42,200 --> 00:17:44,080
This is an example of a book,

319
00:17:44,480 --> 00:17:46,520
which you can see is in pretty bad condition.

320
00:17:47,060 --> 00:17:51,280
It has been infested with what we call bookworms,

321
00:17:51,281 --> 00:17:52,380
which are actually larvae,

322
00:17:52,440 --> 00:17:55,260
and you can see that the damage goes all the way through the book,

323
00:17:55,261 --> 00:17:58,320
and it extends page after page after page.

324
00:17:58,321 --> 00:18:02,320
To prevent this librarian's nightmare,

325
00:18:02,420 --> 00:18:04,920
specialists in the library's preservation labs

326
00:18:05,020 --> 00:18:06,760
work to determine the best conditions

327
00:18:06,860 --> 00:18:07,960
for archival storage.

328
00:18:11,960 --> 00:18:14,360
Environmental scanning electron microscopes

329
00:18:14,460 --> 00:18:18,880
are critical tools in the evaluation process.

330
00:18:18,980 --> 00:18:21,980
We have in here right now, as you can see in this image,

331
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:25,380
we actually have a print in here, and that's the whole print.

332
00:18:25,480 --> 00:18:28,080
And Jennifer is now looking at that print

333
00:18:28,081 --> 00:18:30,080
to see what sorts of materials she can see.

334
00:18:30,081 --> 00:18:32,080
One of the things we're seeing is we're going deep into that

335
00:18:32,081 --> 00:18:36,080
print, and we're actually seeing a cotton fiber here.

336
00:18:36,081 --> 00:18:39,080
Scientists can change the climate inside the

337
00:18:39,081 --> 00:18:43,080
environmental chamber to determine the effects of heat

338
00:18:43,081 --> 00:18:45,080
and humidity on the material.

339
00:18:45,081 --> 00:18:49,080
Every form of paper has its own comfort zone.

340
00:18:49,081 --> 00:18:52,080
The results are then incorporated into real-world storage

341
00:18:52,081 --> 00:18:55,080
of the library treasures.

342
00:18:55,081 --> 00:18:58,780
Roughly half of the entire library collections, over 60

343
00:18:58,880 --> 00:19:02,780
million items, are stored in the Manuscript Division, row

344
00:19:02,880 --> 00:19:05,820
after row after row of stacks containing the writings of

345
00:19:05,920 --> 00:19:10,620
Supreme Court justices, cabinet members, and of course,

346
00:19:10,720 --> 00:19:13,740
presidents.

347
00:19:13,840 --> 00:19:16,640
Here we have the diaries of George Washington.

348
00:19:16,740 --> 00:19:19,340
This is just a part of our very large collection.

349
00:19:19,440 --> 00:19:22,780
Washington's fairly consistently writing about the

350
00:19:22,781 --> 00:19:25,120
about the weather and the crops.

351
00:19:25,121 --> 00:19:28,120
It's clear morning, rather cool.

352
00:19:28,121 --> 00:19:32,280
Then it's still clear, and it starts raining.

353
00:19:32,281 --> 00:19:35,660
And this is characteristic of most of the entries in the diary.

354
00:19:35,661 --> 00:19:38,760
As I say, you don't have any great travails of the soul

355
00:19:38,761 --> 00:19:41,000
or in pouring out his guts, really,

356
00:19:41,001 --> 00:19:44,140
about the agony of some decision he has to make

357
00:19:44,141 --> 00:19:46,140
or anything of that sort.

358
00:19:46,141 --> 00:19:48,440
Now we're in the Theodore Roosevelt collection.

359
00:19:48,441 --> 00:19:51,140
There's a real favorite of mine in here.

360
00:19:51,141 --> 00:19:54,040
This is Theodore Roosevelt's diary,

361
00:19:54,140 --> 00:19:58,940
and it records a devastating day in Roosevelt's life,

362
00:19:59,040 --> 00:20:03,680
the day on which his mother and young wife died,

363
00:20:03,780 --> 00:20:05,880
both on the same day.

364
00:20:05,980 --> 00:20:09,600
He dramatically puts this black accent and says,

365
00:20:09,700 --> 00:20:13,200
the light has gone out of my life.

366
00:20:13,300 --> 00:20:15,940
Some of the most valued items in the manuscript collection

367
00:20:16,040 --> 00:20:19,240
are not handwritten diaries or letters,

368
00:20:19,241 --> 00:20:21,840
but telegrams.

369
00:20:21,940 --> 00:20:25,840
A telegram from Orville Wright to his father

370
00:20:25,940 --> 00:20:30,080
from Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, December 17, 1903,

371
00:20:30,180 --> 00:20:33,580
describing the first flight.

372
00:20:33,680 --> 00:20:38,600
Talks about the average speed to the air was 35 miles per hour,

373
00:20:38,700 --> 00:20:42,400
and they were in the air for 57 seconds.

374
00:20:42,500 --> 00:20:47,140
And he asks his father to inform the press.

375
00:20:47,141 --> 00:20:49,680
Handwritten manuscripts are high on the priority list

376
00:20:49,780 --> 00:20:52,640
for scanning, as the digital copies

377
00:20:52,740 --> 00:20:55,340
provide archival sources as well as opportunities

378
00:20:55,440 --> 00:20:57,880
for viewing on the internet.

379
00:20:57,980 --> 00:21:01,080
These are the original documents handwritten by Abraham Lincoln,

380
00:21:01,180 --> 00:21:05,900
as well as senators, generals, anyone, any correspondence.

381
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:09,200
What we're doing is we're taking the 204 volumes with all the

382
00:21:09,300 --> 00:21:12,800
items that were attached in 1950s in a book form.

383
00:21:12,900 --> 00:21:14,840
And we're going to take those, and we're going to digitize

384
00:21:14,841 --> 00:21:19,840
so they can be presented on the web and maybe reprint it.

385
00:21:19,940 --> 00:21:22,080
Manuscripts in the library's music division

386
00:21:22,180 --> 00:21:26,080
are also one-of-a-kind historic treasures.

387
00:21:26,180 --> 00:21:28,780
Original handwritten compositions of Beethoven

388
00:21:28,880 --> 00:21:33,260
and Mozart provide insight into the composer's personalities.

389
00:21:33,360 --> 00:21:36,120
There's sort of a popular view of Beethoven

390
00:21:36,220 --> 00:21:38,400
that says his hair was always messy.

391
00:21:38,500 --> 00:21:39,760
He had a scowl on his face.

392
00:21:39,860 --> 00:21:42,460
He was in a bad mood and in a hurry all the time.

393
00:21:42,461 --> 00:21:46,460
And if you want to put that image onto this manuscript,

394
00:21:46,560 --> 00:21:48,860
I think you can sort of see it here.

395
00:21:48,960 --> 00:21:52,540
I mean, it looks like conventional musical notation,

396
00:21:52,640 --> 00:21:56,700
but it's definitely written off in a hurry.

397
00:21:56,800 --> 00:21:58,980
We have ink blots here and there.

398
00:21:59,080 --> 00:22:02,240
We have Beethoven changing his mind as he goes.

399
00:22:04,180 --> 00:22:06,520
Mozart, on the other hand, had a reputation

400
00:22:06,620 --> 00:22:10,580
for knowing exactly where he wanted to go.

401
00:22:10,581 --> 00:22:13,780
To look at this manuscript and see how tiny the notes are,

402
00:22:13,880 --> 00:22:17,280
how precisely they're written, it's absolutely clear

403
00:22:17,380 --> 00:22:21,020
whether the notes are on a line or whether they're on a space.

404
00:22:21,120 --> 00:22:25,200
The notations, no matter how small, are extremely legible,

405
00:22:25,300 --> 00:22:31,240
and there's almost no corrections or changes

406
00:22:31,340 --> 00:22:33,240
or alterations in this score.

407
00:22:35,940 --> 00:22:37,700
Should a visitor to the library wish

408
00:22:37,701 --> 00:22:40,700
wish to follow up an inspection of a composer's manuscript

409
00:22:40,800 --> 00:22:42,700
with a look at the actual instruments

410
00:22:42,800 --> 00:22:46,700
on which it might have been played.

411
00:22:46,800 --> 00:22:48,840
They're right down the hall.

412
00:22:48,940 --> 00:22:51,540
The Library of Congress has the world's largest collection

413
00:22:51,640 --> 00:22:56,560
of flutes, more than 1,700, made from crystal, wood,

414
00:22:56,660 --> 00:22:58,660
and even solid gold.

415
00:22:58,760 --> 00:23:01,960
The instruments donated by a private collector

416
00:23:02,060 --> 00:23:07,700
include flutes owned by James Madison and Frederick the Great.

417
00:23:07,701 --> 00:23:12,140
The music division's own holy grail is its collection of

418
00:23:12,240 --> 00:23:15,900
six string instruments manufactured by Stradivarius.

419
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:19,640
Three violins, a cello, and two violas.

420
00:23:22,940 --> 00:23:25,560
Several times each year, they're played in concert.

421
00:23:28,160 --> 00:23:31,560
Library historians have had so many requests to inspect the

422
00:23:31,660 --> 00:23:34,100
instruments to determine the secrets of their brilliant

423
00:23:34,200 --> 00:23:35,200
sound.

424
00:23:35,201 --> 00:23:38,200
They performed CAT scans to produce definitive, precise

425
00:23:38,201 --> 00:23:39,200
dimensions.

426
00:23:41,200 --> 00:23:43,200
We can study the measurements.

427
00:23:43,201 --> 00:23:47,200
We can study the inside of it, the air on the inside, the

428
00:23:47,201 --> 00:23:48,200
density of the wood.

429
00:23:48,201 --> 00:23:52,200
And while I don't know yet what it has told us, I think we can

430
00:23:52,201 --> 00:23:56,200
leave that for scientists and people to study in the future.

431
00:23:56,201 --> 00:23:59,200
But as a curator, it gives me a fingerprint.

432
00:23:59,201 --> 00:24:02,200
It gives me the exact dimensions.

433
00:24:02,201 --> 00:24:05,400
It tells me what's happened to this instrument up until the

434
00:24:05,500 --> 00:24:06,500
time of the scan.

435
00:24:06,600 --> 00:24:08,140
And then if I scan it in the future,

436
00:24:08,240 --> 00:24:11,540
I can see how it might have changed.

437
00:24:11,640 --> 00:24:13,440
The Library of Congress is determined

438
00:24:13,540 --> 00:24:17,940
to preserve American and world musical history in every form,

439
00:24:18,040 --> 00:24:20,760
including recordings.

440
00:24:20,860 --> 00:24:24,060
Today, they even perform tests to determine how long your own

441
00:24:24,160 --> 00:24:27,500
CD collection will last.

442
00:24:27,600 --> 00:24:30,400
Prepare to be surprised.

443
00:24:32,200 --> 00:24:38,600
In the preservation labs of the Library of Congress,

444
00:24:38,601 --> 00:24:41,640
scientists examine every possible scenario that

445
00:24:41,641 --> 00:24:45,760
can lead to the deterioration or destruction of every form

446
00:24:45,761 --> 00:24:50,000
of recorded media, from vinyl disks left in less than ideal

447
00:24:50,001 --> 00:24:52,920
condition to audio tapes that have lost their magnetic

448
00:24:52,921 --> 00:24:55,240
coatings.

449
00:24:55,241 --> 00:24:59,160
Researchers use accelerated aging to determine how well and how

450
00:24:59,161 --> 00:25:01,000
various forms of media will last.

451
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,160
With hundreds of millions of compact discs

452
00:25:07,260 --> 00:25:10,780
in private music collections, the CD was a natural choice

453
00:25:10,880 --> 00:25:12,480
for an accelerated aging comparison.

454
00:25:15,080 --> 00:25:16,880
First, the good news.

455
00:25:16,980 --> 00:25:19,640
This CD passed the test with flying colors,

456
00:25:19,740 --> 00:25:22,380
losing virtually no data.

457
00:25:22,480 --> 00:25:25,480
But this identical disc from the same manufacturer

458
00:25:25,580 --> 00:25:27,380
lost everything.

459
00:25:27,480 --> 00:25:28,420
Why?

460
00:25:28,421 --> 00:25:32,260
Manufacturers use different components

461
00:25:32,360 --> 00:25:34,860
when they make these things, and they change the formulas

462
00:25:34,960 --> 00:25:37,460
all the time for various reasons, for economic reasons,

463
00:25:37,560 --> 00:25:39,660
for efficiency reasons, and so forth.

464
00:25:39,760 --> 00:25:42,260
And they don't tell the consumer when they change the materials

465
00:25:42,360 --> 00:25:44,660
necessarily and the composition.

466
00:25:44,760 --> 00:25:46,680
And there's no way that you as a consumer

467
00:25:46,780 --> 00:25:49,280
could tell the difference.

468
00:25:49,380 --> 00:25:51,680
Instruments in the library's preservation lab

469
00:25:51,780 --> 00:25:55,620
are able to differentiate data pits, the binary holes in a CD

470
00:25:55,621 --> 00:26:00,620
that creates sound from pinholes, defects caused

471
00:26:00,720 --> 00:26:04,620
when protective layers of lacquer degrade over time,

472
00:26:04,720 --> 00:26:06,860
causing the loss of sound.

473
00:26:06,960 --> 00:26:08,600
In this case, you can see there are a number

474
00:26:08,700 --> 00:26:10,640
of different types of defects.

475
00:26:10,740 --> 00:26:14,980
The ImagePro can count them, measure them,

476
00:26:15,080 --> 00:26:17,840
gives a distribution here where you're seeing this count

477
00:26:17,940 --> 00:26:21,820
how many of each size that you have.

478
00:26:21,920 --> 00:26:25,620
Nitrocellulose coatings used prior to 1994

479
00:26:25,621 --> 00:26:30,360
are more prone to data loss than those used on newer CDs.

480
00:26:30,460 --> 00:26:33,760
But still, there are no guarantees.

481
00:26:33,860 --> 00:26:36,660
You can have a CD of roughly the same age,

482
00:26:36,760 --> 00:26:40,660
and one will have deteriorated very seriously if it has had,

483
00:26:40,760 --> 00:26:42,540
say, this nitrocellulose coating,

484
00:26:42,640 --> 00:26:45,300
and another one of the same age with an acrylic coating

485
00:26:45,400 --> 00:26:48,640
might be in perfect shape.

486
00:26:48,740 --> 00:26:52,120
During the past 20 years, Library of Congress

487
00:26:52,220 --> 00:26:55,620
scientists have used every form of archival digital storage

488
00:26:55,621 --> 00:26:59,620
Tapes, CDs, hard drives, and hard media when scanning

489
00:26:59,720 --> 00:27:01,960
library collections.

490
00:27:02,060 --> 00:27:05,300
In the division of prints and photographs,

491
00:27:05,400 --> 00:27:08,360
images are scanned each day using cameras capable of

492
00:27:08,460 --> 00:27:12,840
recording up to 88 megapixels, 10 times the number of most

493
00:27:12,940 --> 00:27:15,280
consumer models.

494
00:27:15,380 --> 00:27:18,280
The scan normally takes about a minute from writing

495
00:27:18,380 --> 00:27:21,040
all the way to completion of the scan.

496
00:27:21,041 --> 00:27:25,580
We can roughly get through about 100 prints a day,

497
00:27:25,680 --> 00:27:30,680
sometimes up to 130 negatives.

498
00:27:30,780 --> 00:27:33,180
With the longevity of digital archival storage

499
00:27:33,280 --> 00:27:36,680
still unpredictable, protection of original sources

500
00:27:36,780 --> 00:27:41,500
remains a priority, especially for priceless historic

501
00:27:41,600 --> 00:27:43,800
original photographs.

502
00:27:43,900 --> 00:27:46,640
Many of the most treasured images are valued because they

503
00:27:46,740 --> 00:27:51,040
represent landmarks of technological change.

504
00:27:51,041 --> 00:27:54,880
Daguerreotypes marked the transition from sketches

505
00:27:54,881 --> 00:27:57,880
to photographs.

506
00:27:57,881 --> 00:28:01,480
While he was president, Nathaniel Currier

507
00:28:01,481 --> 00:28:04,380
hearkened back to Andrew Jackson's fame

508
00:28:04,381 --> 00:28:07,680
as the hero of New Orleans during the War of 1812.

509
00:28:07,681 --> 00:28:10,800
It's a symbolic portraiture.

510
00:28:10,801 --> 00:28:12,800
But after he's finished being president,

511
00:28:12,801 --> 00:28:17,300
he agreed to let the hot new technology capture his visage.

512
00:28:17,301 --> 00:28:20,940
Even though he was frail and feeble,

513
00:28:21,040 --> 00:28:24,640
he's being supported in the chair with pillows behind him.

514
00:28:24,740 --> 00:28:30,440
He was willing to let the world know what he really looked like.

515
00:28:30,540 --> 00:28:32,540
This aerial photo of San Francisco

516
00:28:32,640 --> 00:28:36,160
following the devastating 1906 earthquake

517
00:28:36,260 --> 00:28:39,160
is another gem of the library collection.

518
00:28:39,260 --> 00:28:42,260
It was taken by photographer George Lawrence,

519
00:28:42,360 --> 00:28:46,360
who flew his cameras on kites.

520
00:28:46,361 --> 00:28:49,360
The air currents were sufficient to take his kites up

521
00:28:49,460 --> 00:28:51,360
to different heights.

522
00:28:51,460 --> 00:28:54,360
He sold the prints, I've read.

523
00:28:54,460 --> 00:28:58,100
They were phenomenal in their day, incredibly popular.

524
00:28:58,200 --> 00:29:02,040
He made at least $15,000, which is quite a piece of change

525
00:29:02,140 --> 00:29:04,280
in 1906.

526
00:29:04,380 --> 00:29:06,780
Other photos are valued because they literally brought

527
00:29:06,880 --> 00:29:09,680
about social change.

528
00:29:09,780 --> 00:29:12,680
We have a collection of 5,000 photographs

529
00:29:12,780 --> 00:29:15,660
that document child labor in the United States

530
00:29:15,661 --> 00:29:17,560
around 1910.

531
00:29:17,660 --> 00:29:19,260
It's one thing to read about it.

532
00:29:19,360 --> 00:29:23,960
It's a whole other experience to look in those children's faces

533
00:29:24,060 --> 00:29:26,540
and think about, is it all right for children

534
00:29:26,640 --> 00:29:29,340
to continue to work this hard this long?

535
00:29:29,440 --> 00:29:32,140
These pictures helped to change many of the state laws

536
00:29:32,240 --> 00:29:34,880
on child labor or helped to make sure

537
00:29:34,980 --> 00:29:39,120
that the existing laws were enforced.

538
00:29:39,220 --> 00:29:41,260
Some photos are considered extraordinary

539
00:29:41,360 --> 00:29:44,860
for their beauty and their size.

540
00:29:44,861 --> 00:29:46,960
This book of images is the largest volume

541
00:29:47,060 --> 00:29:49,760
in the Library of Congress.

542
00:29:49,860 --> 00:29:53,360
It's called Bhutan, A Visual Odyssey.

543
00:29:53,460 --> 00:29:55,600
I'm going to get some help to open it,

544
00:29:55,700 --> 00:29:59,500
because it's five feet by seven feet.

545
00:29:59,600 --> 00:30:05,920
I've been told that it took a football field length of paper

546
00:30:06,020 --> 00:30:08,120
in order to print an entire volume.

547
00:30:11,020 --> 00:30:13,820
Hidden away in the Prints and Photographs Division

548
00:30:13,821 --> 00:30:16,820
are also architectural drawings,

549
00:30:16,821 --> 00:30:21,820
including submissions for the design of the Vietnam Memorial.

550
00:30:21,821 --> 00:30:23,820
You can see from how slowly I'm going

551
00:30:23,821 --> 00:30:26,820
that this is a very heavy board.

552
00:30:26,821 --> 00:30:30,820
It's 30 by 40 inches.

553
00:30:30,821 --> 00:30:35,820
And lo and behold, the winning design competition drawing

554
00:30:35,821 --> 00:30:39,820
by Maya Lin to build the Vietnam Veterans Memorial

555
00:30:39,821 --> 00:30:41,820
in Washington, D.C.

556
00:30:41,821 --> 00:30:45,720
This is one of more than 1,400 competition entries

557
00:30:45,820 --> 00:30:47,960
that the commission received.

558
00:30:48,060 --> 00:30:51,160
On the lower level of the library's Madison Building

559
00:30:51,260 --> 00:30:53,960
is the Geography and Map Division,

560
00:30:54,060 --> 00:30:59,040
a collection of more than 5 million maps and 80,000 atlases.

561
00:30:59,140 --> 00:31:02,140
Hundreds are considered treasures,

562
00:31:02,240 --> 00:31:04,980
but two routinely leave historians and geographers

563
00:31:05,080 --> 00:31:07,840
speechless.

564
00:31:07,841 --> 00:31:14,640
This map came from Spanish, English, and French sources.

565
00:31:15,280 --> 00:31:21,240
And when Thomas Jefferson was preparing for his Lewis and Clark expedition, he knew of this map.

566
00:31:21,880 --> 00:31:27,980
So literally, you're looking at a map that Lewis and Clark took with them on the trail and brought back.

567
00:31:28,120 --> 00:31:30,160
That's a rarity of itself.

568
00:31:30,161 --> 00:31:34,900
But this is a map that actually provides a triptych for the first

569
00:31:35,000 --> 00:31:39,500
year of their expedition to the Great Falls in Montana.

570
00:31:39,600 --> 00:31:42,400
And the second most extraordinary map?

571
00:31:42,500 --> 00:31:47,980
One that took a total of 39 years to complete, hand-drawn

572
00:31:48,080 --> 00:31:52,080
by George Washington, depicting the property he owned in Mount

573
00:31:52,180 --> 00:31:53,280
Vernon.

574
00:31:53,380 --> 00:31:56,560
So we have one map that transcends a very active time

575
00:31:56,660 --> 00:31:58,560
in American history.

576
00:31:58,561 --> 00:32:00,860
This is another way to view George Washington.

577
00:32:00,960 --> 00:32:02,360
He is a mapmaker.

578
00:32:02,460 --> 00:32:05,200
He is a cartographer, the first mapmaker.

579
00:32:08,700 --> 00:32:11,100
In the library's preservation department,

580
00:32:11,200 --> 00:32:13,800
other maps are given new life with the help

581
00:32:13,900 --> 00:32:16,220
of 21st century technology.

582
00:32:16,320 --> 00:32:21,620
This plan for Washington, DC, was hand-drawn in 1791

583
00:32:21,720 --> 00:32:24,520
by architect Pierre L'Enfant.

584
00:32:24,620 --> 00:32:27,760
When digitally photographed with spectral imaging technology,

585
00:32:27,761 --> 00:32:31,760
technology.

586
00:32:31,761 --> 00:32:36,660
Infrared light brings out faded details.

587
00:32:36,661 --> 00:32:39,740
What that does is it brings out a lot of information

588
00:32:39,741 --> 00:32:42,680
from the document that we can't see with the naked eye.

589
00:32:42,681 --> 00:32:44,280
This is what we see under the infrared.

590
00:32:44,281 --> 00:32:47,380
So you can see that wonderful grid of the streets,

591
00:32:47,381 --> 00:32:50,320
of details, of pencil annotations.

592
00:32:50,321 --> 00:32:52,480
It keeps popping up to you.

593
00:32:52,481 --> 00:32:55,680
And as we come in here, you can see the president's house.

594
00:32:55,681 --> 00:32:57,980
You can see I've just circled some areas here

595
00:32:57,981 --> 00:33:00,360
that we want to go in and look at in more detail,

596
00:33:00,361 --> 00:33:04,320
which were originally trees and other planned areas.

597
00:33:04,321 --> 00:33:06,700
You can see Congress over here.

598
00:33:06,701 --> 00:33:09,620
And we can start to bring out the information

599
00:33:09,621 --> 00:33:12,200
that had been lost there.

600
00:33:12,201 --> 00:33:14,760
Preserving the nation's historic treasures

601
00:33:14,761 --> 00:33:18,400
is a major challenge for the Library of Congress.

602
00:33:18,401 --> 00:33:23,500
But new technologies bring new potential every day.

603
00:33:23,501 --> 00:33:26,540
The library's sophisticated ultra-safe storage system

604
00:33:26,541 --> 00:33:29,140
for America's classic motion pictures

605
00:33:29,141 --> 00:33:32,940
ensures that Frankenstein will truly remain alive.

606
00:33:38,140 --> 00:33:42,260
75 miles from Capitol Hill in Culpeper, Virginia,

607
00:33:42,261 --> 00:33:45,520
the Library of Congress's newly completed Packard Campus

608
00:33:45,521 --> 00:33:49,760
for Audiovisual Conservation sits half above ground

609
00:33:49,761 --> 00:33:51,500
and half below.

610
00:33:51,501 --> 00:33:54,740
within a former bomb shelter. The building was designed to

611
00:33:54,840 --> 00:33:57,900
protect its contents from any catastrophic event.

612
00:34:00,240 --> 00:34:03,380
And those contents happened to include the most valuable motion

613
00:34:03,480 --> 00:34:09,480
picture artifacts in the world, including 140,000 reels of

614
00:34:09,580 --> 00:34:14,360
original nitrate camera negatives in prints from America's first

615
00:34:14,460 --> 00:34:20,460
60 years of movie making. We're standing in the Library

616
00:34:20,461 --> 00:34:23,660
the Library of Congress's nitrate film vaults.

617
00:34:23,661 --> 00:34:27,000
50 of those vaults right behind me here, another

618
00:34:27,001 --> 00:34:32,340
74 on the other side of those doors at the end of the hallway, 124 vaults altogether.

619
00:34:33,000 --> 00:34:38,760
Up until about 1951, all theatrical motion pictures that were shown in this country week

620
00:34:38,761 --> 00:34:43,480
in and week out that your parents or your grandparents went to were actually shot on

621
00:34:43,481 --> 00:34:48,589
nitrate film stock. We have the original camera negative of Jimmy Stewart and Mr.

622
00:34:48,590 --> 00:34:49,290
Smith goes

623
00:34:49,291 --> 00:34:52,260
to Washington and all the Frank Capra films.

624
00:34:52,261 --> 00:34:54,460
When I say original camera negative,

625
00:34:54,461 --> 00:34:57,500
that means the film that was in the camera

626
00:34:57,501 --> 00:35:00,140
when Frank Capra had Jimmy Stewart standing

627
00:35:00,141 --> 00:35:01,160
right in front of it.

628
00:35:01,161 --> 00:35:06,340
So that's typical of what we have here.

629
00:35:06,341 --> 00:35:08,880
Just right here, I'm reaching for one reel of film.

630
00:35:08,881 --> 00:35:12,280
This is reel three from the original camera

631
00:35:12,281 --> 00:35:16,440
negative of Frankenstein, 1931 with Boris Karloff.

632
00:35:16,441 --> 00:35:18,280
In fact, reel three is the creation scene

633
00:35:18,281 --> 00:35:23,280
where it's alive is yelled out by Dr. Frankenstein.

634
00:35:23,380 --> 00:35:27,280
Nitrate film stock is highly flammable.

635
00:35:27,380 --> 00:35:30,920
If a reel starts burning, not even immersing it in water

636
00:35:31,020 --> 00:35:34,320
will extinguish the flames, because when nitrate burns,

637
00:35:34,420 --> 00:35:37,840
it creates its own oxygen.

638
00:35:37,940 --> 00:35:40,840
No expense was spared in making the nitrate vaults fireproof.

639
00:35:44,340 --> 00:35:47,740
Should a nitrate fire ever start in a film canister,

640
00:35:47,741 --> 00:35:51,440
it would be contained to one fireproof shelf.

641
00:35:51,540 --> 00:35:54,340
Sprinklers angled from above would cascade water

642
00:35:54,440 --> 00:35:56,180
in front of the shelves to prevent the fire

643
00:35:56,280 --> 00:35:57,180
from spreading outward.

644
00:36:00,380 --> 00:36:02,180
The Packard campus is the new home

645
00:36:02,280 --> 00:36:04,900
of the most precious film negatives and elements donated

646
00:36:05,000 --> 00:36:08,700
to the library by most of Hollywood's major studios.

647
00:36:12,240 --> 00:36:14,840
Because the vault areas are kept at a constant temperature

648
00:36:14,841 --> 00:36:18,840
Fahrenheit and humidity of 30%.

649
00:36:18,940 --> 00:36:22,340
Even in the warmest months, film specialists are dressed

650
00:36:22,440 --> 00:36:23,640
for winter.

651
00:36:23,740 --> 00:36:26,780
There are no complaints considering their everyday

652
00:36:26,880 --> 00:36:30,820
access to movie making's greatest treasures, some famous

653
00:36:30,920 --> 00:36:34,100
and some relatively unknown.

654
00:36:34,200 --> 00:36:35,900
The film I'm looking at right now

655
00:36:36,000 --> 00:36:38,900
is one we consider a jewel in our collection.

656
00:36:39,000 --> 00:36:42,600
It's The Great Train Robbery from 1903.

657
00:36:42,601 --> 00:36:45,840
It was made by the Thomas Edison Studios.

658
00:36:45,841 --> 00:36:49,400
It actually told a story based on a historical fact.

659
00:36:49,401 --> 00:36:54,800
It was filmed in New Jersey, and it was one of the biggest hits of early film.

660
00:36:54,801 --> 00:36:59,560
It kind of made movies that tell a story a success.

661
00:36:59,561 --> 00:37:05,380
This film here is actually a color film, probably made between 1905 and 1910, but they didn't

662
00:37:05,381 --> 00:37:11,280
have a color process that could capture color on film, so it had to be hand-painted.

663
00:37:11,281 --> 00:37:17,060
What I mean by that is it's black and white film stock that was once the film was developed,

664
00:37:17,061 --> 00:37:22,760
they would take it to a warehouse assembly line and women would sit all day with their

665
00:37:22,761 --> 00:37:29,460
color ink and a single hair artist brush and paint in, and you can see it here, the colors

666
00:37:29,461 --> 00:37:33,560
because the title of this film is The Metamorphosis of a Butterfly.

667
00:37:33,561 --> 00:37:35,880
It's a French film.

668
00:37:35,881 --> 00:37:40,300
The same careful handling that films receive in library collections is also extended to

669
00:37:40,301 --> 00:37:44,000
state-of-the-art labs are equipped to make digital copies

670
00:37:44,100 --> 00:37:47,900
of every form of recorded sound,

671
00:37:48,000 --> 00:37:53,420
from wax cylinders of the early 1900s to vinyl discs,

672
00:37:53,520 --> 00:37:57,520
which were replaced by CDs.

673
00:37:57,620 --> 00:37:59,580
This is a Simon York turntable,

674
00:37:59,680 --> 00:38:03,920
and they cost approximately $20,000 a piece.

675
00:38:04,020 --> 00:38:07,320
They allow us to have two different tone arms

676
00:38:07,420 --> 00:38:09,260
for two different styluses and cartridges

677
00:38:09,261 --> 00:38:12,600
and cartridges for doing A-B testing to find out which one

678
00:38:12,700 --> 00:38:15,260
we want to be able to use.

679
00:38:15,360 --> 00:38:18,260
They also allow us to have a very clean signal going in,

680
00:38:18,360 --> 00:38:22,600
plus allowing us to play them back at any number of speeds.

681
00:38:25,400 --> 00:38:28,020
Most motion pictures, television programs,

682
00:38:28,120 --> 00:38:31,020
and audio recordings that have been digitally copied

683
00:38:31,120 --> 00:38:33,020
can be viewed or heard upon request

684
00:38:33,120 --> 00:38:36,920
at the main library on Capitol Hill via fiber optic line.

685
00:38:39,260 --> 00:38:41,500
But throughout the library system,

686
00:38:41,600 --> 00:38:44,000
specialists are continually preparing materials

687
00:38:44,100 --> 00:38:49,260
for online viewing with the help of a mammoth computer,

688
00:38:49,360 --> 00:38:52,000
custom designed to distribute information

689
00:38:52,100 --> 00:38:53,400
to the entire world.

690
00:38:58,920 --> 00:39:01,720
In every department of the Library of Congress,

691
00:39:01,820 --> 00:39:04,560
specialists are scanning the library's greatest treasures

692
00:39:04,660 --> 00:39:08,320
to make them available online.

693
00:39:08,321 --> 00:39:11,560
Many of the newer items are older analog videotapes, which

694
00:39:11,660 --> 00:39:16,160
need to be duplicated into digital formats.

695
00:39:16,260 --> 00:39:18,300
We're doing one particular period

696
00:39:18,400 --> 00:39:22,260
in the history of CBS News, their archive, which is 1974

697
00:39:22,360 --> 00:39:23,900
to 1985.

698
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:29,080
And just in that 11 years, we're talking 800,000 cassettes

699
00:39:29,180 --> 00:39:33,420
of one type, the 3 1⁄4-inch cassette type.

700
00:39:33,520 --> 00:39:36,220
The biggest challenge of being able to make digital copies

701
00:39:36,221 --> 00:39:42,060
of videotapes is to have working machines to play them all back.

702
00:39:42,061 --> 00:39:44,620
For that reason, Culpepper has become one of the largest

703
00:39:44,621 --> 00:39:47,760
collectors of working video and audio machines in the world.

704
00:39:51,160 --> 00:39:55,380
When the appreciation of art forms is machine dependent,

705
00:39:55,381 --> 00:39:58,280
it's critical to make sure at least one appropriate machine

706
00:39:58,281 --> 00:40:02,920
is able to make a transfer to new digital formats.

707
00:40:02,921 --> 00:40:06,320
Packard engineers scour used equipment houses and even

708
00:40:06,420 --> 00:40:12,360
garage sales for spare parts to keep outdated machines working.

709
00:40:12,460 --> 00:40:14,360
We're in the master storage facility

710
00:40:14,460 --> 00:40:17,260
here at the Library of Congress in Culpeper, Virginia.

711
00:40:17,360 --> 00:40:19,380
And what you see around me on my left

712
00:40:19,480 --> 00:40:22,880
is a bunch of old videotape machines, parts, mounting parts,

713
00:40:22,980 --> 00:40:24,380
control panels.

714
00:40:24,480 --> 00:40:29,180
On my right are all the boxes for the spare parts, manuals.

715
00:40:29,280 --> 00:40:31,320
There are boxes up here, the gray boxes you see

716
00:40:31,321 --> 00:40:33,560
for the old two-inch quad machines.

717
00:40:33,660 --> 00:40:36,260
We've got some projection equipment here.

718
00:40:36,360 --> 00:40:37,760
We've got lamp houses.

719
00:40:37,860 --> 00:40:39,260
We've got rewind tables.

720
00:40:39,360 --> 00:40:41,260
You'd be amazed where we find this stuff.

721
00:40:43,760 --> 00:40:46,940
All of the newly digitized material eventually ends up

722
00:40:47,040 --> 00:40:51,900
here, the data center at the library's Packard facility,

723
00:40:52,000 --> 00:40:55,940
in a massive computer system capable of accessing up to 16

724
00:40:56,040 --> 00:40:57,480
petabytes of digital data.

725
00:40:57,481 --> 00:41:00,380
data.

726
00:41:00,480 --> 00:41:01,980
That's the equivalent of the storage

727
00:41:02,080 --> 00:41:08,360
in approximately 160,000 laptop computers.

728
00:41:08,460 --> 00:41:10,960
What you're seeing with the robotic systems

729
00:41:11,060 --> 00:41:16,340
within the tape system, those will grab one of these tapes

730
00:41:16,440 --> 00:41:18,800
and they will take it to a playback drive,

731
00:41:18,900 --> 00:41:21,880
and that drive will play back the data on that tape.

732
00:41:21,980 --> 00:41:26,080
It is a very large jukebox for the people of the United States

733
00:41:26,081 --> 00:41:33,080
to play back the past. In April 2009, under the

734
00:41:33,081 --> 00:41:36,920
guidance of Librarian of Congress Dr. James Billington,

735
00:41:36,921 --> 00:41:40,520
the library's online presence made a major leap into the 21st

736
00:41:40,521 --> 00:41:47,840
century with the launch of the World Digital Library, WDL.org,

737
00:41:47,841 --> 00:41:51,440
a UNESCO project enabling nations to share cultural

738
00:41:51,441 --> 00:41:54,040
resources.

739
00:41:54,140 --> 00:41:59,340
In its first week, the website received 16 million page views.

740
00:41:59,440 --> 00:42:03,260
For Dr. Billington, it's an encouraging sign that people

741
00:42:03,360 --> 00:42:07,560
of every nation have a hunger to learn about each other.

742
00:42:07,660 --> 00:42:12,420
If more people have more access to more knowledge and creativity

743
00:42:12,520 --> 00:42:18,300
and use it in more and more ways, whatever the problems of today,

744
00:42:18,301 --> 00:42:19,940
Learning about it, studying about it,

745
00:42:20,040 --> 00:42:22,840
getting interested in it, can always make tomorrow

746
00:42:22,940 --> 00:42:24,740
a little bit better than yesterday.

747
00:42:24,840 --> 00:42:27,040
But unless you know what happened yesterday,

748
00:42:27,140 --> 00:42:31,040
you can't create a new tomorrow.

749
00:42:31,140 --> 00:42:33,440
When Thomas Jefferson sold his personal library

750
00:42:33,540 --> 00:42:35,760
to Congress, he could never have foreseen

751
00:42:35,860 --> 00:42:38,660
that a collection of roughly 6,500 items

752
00:42:38,760 --> 00:42:43,460
would grow into 144 million.

753
00:42:43,560 --> 00:42:44,900
He could certainly not have imagined

754
00:42:45,000 --> 00:42:48,300
an innovation like the internet that could not

755
00:42:48,301 --> 00:42:52,700
available instantly to billions. But he thoroughly understood

756
00:42:52,701 --> 00:42:56,700
the long-term purpose of a Library of Congress, to serve as

757
00:42:56,701 --> 00:43:01,540
a torch of enlightenment. Now in the digital age, that

758
00:43:01,541 --> 00:43:03,540
torch will light the world.

